Jonathan Sobel: So… Is that a 1.0 or a post 1.0?

72
00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:37.040
Kent Dybvig: I think that's post 1.0.

73
00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:41.890
Kent Dybvig: The real… the real key with 1.0 is to get all the braking changes in.

74
00:07:42.710 --> 00:07:44.959
Kent Dybvig: Cause then, once we…

75
00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:52.829
Kent Dybvig: once we have 1.0, we have to start a deprecation process for changes. We have to have a back compatibility story.

76
00:07:53.510 --> 00:07:57.339
Kent Dybvig: Up to 1.0, we're… we're not…

77
00:07:58.040 --> 00:08:01.739
Kent Dybvig: You know, we're making everything… allowing braking changes.

78
00:08:03.610 --> 00:08:14.020
Kent Dybvig: That said, I don't want to… I don't want to have random braking changes that we can avoid at this point, because, you know, we already have people programming against it, so we're trying to be… we're trying to be conscious of that. I think…

79
00:08:14.250 --> 00:08:22.140
Kent Dybvig: I think the type system changes for JubJubScaler are going to possibly be a pain point for users.

80
00:08:24.220 --> 00:08:28.400
Jonathan Sobel: But the idea is that adding new function is just adding new function.

81
00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:28.890
Kent Dybvig: Yes.

82
00:08:28.890 --> 00:08:40.340
Jonathan Sobel: the scalar thing is changing subtyping rules, and so that's… even though it sounds like a small thing, when you just say, let's just add jub-jub scalers, the fact that it's changing subtyping could break arbitrary code.

83
00:08:40.850 --> 00:08:43.130
Kent Dybvig: Yeah, it's just… I mean, we're…

84
00:08:43.679 --> 00:08:54.459
Kent Dybvig: Along with this, we've decided that you… that it is… that you cannot… Omit casts between… UNTs and fields.

85
00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:59.239
Kent Dybvig: So that a field is not a subtype of Uint, or any Uint type.

86
00:08:59.630 --> 00:09:08.200
Kent Dybvig: sorry, field is not a supertype of UN for any Uint type, because it's not very supportable.

87
00:09:08.590 --> 00:09:12.350
Kent Dybvig: For arbitrary… Scalers.

88
00:09:13.900 --> 00:09:17.980
Kent Dybvig: And, so that's the one that could cause some pain.

89
00:09:18.690 --> 00:09:27.130
Kent Dybvig: On the other hand, eventually we'll… we will allow arbitrary Uint types,

90
00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:36.710
Kent Dybvig: And, yeah.

91
00:09:38.440 --> 00:09:43.790
Kent Dybvig: So… We'll also be able to provide a fix-up tool.

92
00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:49.770
Kent Dybvig: That will, insert the,

93
00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:58.240
Kent Dybvig: The cast to field, if necessary, on… On… on things that, that don't already have them.

94
00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:00.510
Kent Dybvig: And need them.

95
00:10:05.830 --> 00:10:07.270
Parisa Ataei: Joe, go ahead.

96
00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:17.339
Joseph Denman: Yeah, just to answer Jonathan's question, relationship of Compact 1.0 to other signing, Inigo says that we're gonna have to do…

97
00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:36.350
Joseph Denman: After we add store signatures, we're adding RSA and ECDSA, and those are predicated on merging SECP256K1 and the big Uint data types. So I get the impression that this is on the docket for Q2, as a deliverable, so I don't…

98
00:10:36.610 --> 00:10:42.109
Joseph Denman: you know, I don't think that we're going to wait until the end of Q2 to declare Compact 1.0.

99
00:10:42.950 --> 00:10:44.279
Joseph Denman: We could, but…

100
00:10:46.180 --> 00:10:51.130
Kent Dybvig: Well, I think… yeah, we need to get those things in as soon as possible,

101
00:10:51.460 --> 00:10:58.460
Kent Dybvig: The… the… they are the highest priority items on our… on our list, but…

102
00:10:58.650 --> 00:11:04.169
Kent Dybvig: So, we're not gonna wait till the end of Q2 to deliver on the signatures, I don't think, either, but…

103
00:11:05.630 --> 00:11:11.059
Kent Dybvig: But yeah, Jonathan hit the nail on the head. It's just, if it's a new functionality, it's okay to…

104
00:11:11.400 --> 00:11:17.449
Kent Dybvig: it'll be backward compatible. If it's something that changes our existing type system, that's…

105
00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:20.200
Kent Dybvig: That's not, so… we hope that…

106
00:11:20.550 --> 00:11:24.100
Kent Dybvig: Adding these things will not change our…

107
00:11:24.260 --> 00:11:27.250
Kent Dybvig: language. That's part of the reason we're making the change now, is…

108
00:11:27.500 --> 00:11:30.789
Kent Dybvig: Make it less likely that we'll have to change it later.

109
00:11:33.140 --> 00:11:45.380
Kent Dybvig: I'm also working on a proposal for backward compatibility which is basically, that…

110
00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:49.190
Kent Dybvig: We're gonna add a version number to library imports.

111
00:11:50.330 --> 00:11:57.239
Kent Dybvig: And the version numbers to modules, and version numbers to library… to module imports.

112
00:11:57.980 --> 00:12:02.820
Kent Dybvig: That'll be kind of like the Pragma version numbers for language version and compiler version.

113
00:12:03.400 --> 00:12:05.290
Kent Dybvig: So that we can…

114
00:12:05.400 --> 00:12:11.730
Kent Dybvig: maybe we can make breaking changes to the standard library, and then I'm also thinking about some sort of syntactic

115
00:12:12.770 --> 00:12:15.340
Kent Dybvig: Flag.

116
00:12:15.780 --> 00:12:28.010
Kent Dybvig: That allows you to specify that you want the 1.1 version of the parser, as opposed to the 1.0 version, which is always going to be the default as long as we're on 1.x.

117
00:12:29.560 --> 00:12:39.889
Kent Dybvig: as long as we're on 1.X, you get the 1.0 version of the parser, and the keywords it supports, and the reserve words it requires, it has, and all that. But as soon as you,

118
00:12:40.700 --> 00:12:41.790
Kent Dybvig: need…

119
00:12:42.500 --> 00:12:48.769
Kent Dybvig: support for something only on 1.1, you can… you'll be able to have a syntactic flag that says, yeah, for this file.

120
00:12:49.480 --> 00:12:53.339
Kent Dybvig: I want to use the, the 1.1 parser.

121
00:12:54.780 --> 00:12:59.160
Parisa Ataei: Do you… is that a… do you want that to go in before 1.0?

122
00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:08.850
Kent Dybvig: So, I would like to get the import… so yeah, I should write this down. I would like to get the import version numbers in before that.

123
00:13:09.520 --> 00:13:15.470
Kent Dybvig: And actually, the flag, too, would be a good idea, so yeah, I should get off my… No one.

124
00:13:16.270 --> 00:13:18.860
Kent Dybvig: Stuff, and do that stuff as fast as possible.

125
00:13:20.130 --> 00:13:23.490
Parisa Ataei: That's flagged for what?

126
00:13:23.490 --> 00:13:25.610
Kent Dybvig: Parser flag, just call it parser flag.

127
00:13:27.470 --> 00:13:29.130
Kent Dybvig: We'll call it something different.

128
00:13:30.200 --> 00:13:32.519
Kent Dybvig: It'll be a syntax flag or something like that.

129
00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:43.550
Kent Dybvig: So every, every release of… compact, every new…

130
00:13:43.770 --> 00:13:48.519
Kent Dybvig: piece release, you know, like, 1.1 will have its own

131
00:13:49.830 --> 00:13:59.149
Kent Dybvig: it might have its own differing… well, it will. It might be the same, but it'll have its own parser that might be the same as the preceding version.

132
00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:05.409
Kent Dybvig: And it'll have its own standard library that might be the same, and will certainly overlap with, at least.

133
00:14:05.960 --> 00:14:07.350
Kent Dybvig: The preceding version.

134
00:14:16.080 --> 00:14:20.010
Kent Dybvig: And then with 2.0.0, we'll start with a clean slate.

135
00:14:22.520 --> 00:14:27.790
Jonathan Sobel: Would the idea be that you can have the same kind of operators and version numbers that we have in the Pragma?

136
00:14:28.320 --> 00:14:29.000
Kent Dybvig: Yes.

137
00:14:29.670 --> 00:14:31.339
Kent Dybvig: That would be the idea, although I'd…

138
00:14:31.480 --> 00:14:36.969
Kent Dybvig: I don't know if it's necessary, but I think it's… Probably the easiest and…

139
00:14:37.390 --> 00:14:40.189
Kent Dybvig: To explain, and the easiest to implement in a way, so…

140
00:14:41.600 --> 00:14:46.699
Jonathan Sobel: I mean, I can certainly do… imagine doing an import and say I need at least version 2 of this library.

141
00:14:47.380 --> 00:14:48.140
Kent Dybvig: Yeah.

142
00:14:53.510 --> 00:15:02.249
Kent Dybvig: Yeah, in a way, this could supplant uses of the pragma. It may be that the pragma's not going to be so useful after having this.

143
00:15:03.700 --> 00:15:06.560
Kent Dybvig: The parser one, I don't know. I don't know.

144
00:15:08.640 --> 00:15:12.210
Kent Dybvig: If that should be version numbers, or if it should just be…

145
00:15:12.410 --> 00:15:17.460
Kent Dybvig: You know, hash 1.1, hash syntax 1.1, or something like that.

146
00:15:19.130 --> 00:15:24.869
Kent Dybvig: I'm a little… I haven't… I haven't really… Decided what that should be.

147
00:15:25.120 --> 00:15:27.350
Kent Dybvig: Definitely open suggestions there.

148
00:15:28.540 --> 00:15:30.169
Kent Dybvig: It could be the same thing.

149
00:15:32.030 --> 00:15:36.350
Kent Dybvig: Hash syntax, open friend, some version.

150
00:15:37.580 --> 00:15:38.780
Kent Dybvig: expression.

151
00:15:40.050 --> 00:15:44.780
Kent Dybvig: So you could say hash syntax, open front, greater than or equal to 1.1.

152
00:15:55.380 --> 00:15:58.450
Kent Dybvig: I'm a little suspicious of the one… the greater than or equal.

153
00:15:59.590 --> 00:16:02.860
Kent Dybvig: even for the Pragmas, Oh.

154
00:16:06.490 --> 00:16:07.390
Jonathan Sobel: Yeah, those.

155
00:16:07.390 --> 00:16:08.420
Kent Dybvig: is.

156
00:16:08.420 --> 00:16:23.100
Jonathan Sobel: people say, I want something greater than 1.2, and what they really mean is… and nothing beyond 1.5 exists right now, so anything is fine. But then when version 3.0 comes out, it's breaking, and their thing is wrong.

157
00:16:23.920 --> 00:16:24.560
Kent Dybvig: Yeah.

158
00:16:24.690 --> 00:16:25.890
Kent Dybvig: Exactly.

159
00:16:27.390 --> 00:16:30.690
Kent Dybvig: I had this notion, and… M…

160
00:16:32.180 --> 00:16:35.669
Kent Dybvig: Shay Scheme object files that they… that…

161
00:16:35.950 --> 00:16:42.129
Kent Dybvig: The object file would say what version of the compiler, it's compatible with.

162
00:16:42.610 --> 00:16:44.740
Kent Dybvig: And it would have its own version number.

163
00:16:45.010 --> 00:16:53.639
Kent Dybvig: And the compiler would say what versions of the object file format is it compatible with? And they would both have to match in order for it to work.

164
00:16:54.290 --> 00:16:57.049
Kent Dybvig: But that's difficult for us to do in this context.

165
00:16:57.690 --> 00:17:00.549
Kent Dybvig: We don't really… we can't really say that for sure.

166
00:17:02.020 --> 00:17:13.510
Kent Dybvig: about the user's code. We can… we have complete control over the object code and filer, and we can make those decisions, but the user has to make those decisions here, so that's why we have the flexibility, but it's…

167
00:17:14.569 --> 00:17:19.249
Kent Dybvig: You know… It's not like it's gonna work in all cases.

168
00:17:19.380 --> 00:17:20.950
Kent Dybvig: To use the greater than or equal.

169
00:17:28.180 --> 00:17:29.620
Parisa Ataei: Okay.

170
00:17:30.630 --> 00:17:32.170
Parisa Ataei: Any other changes?

171
00:17:37.730 --> 00:17:38.740
Parisa Ataei: All right.

172
00:17:39.120 --> 00:17:46.399
Parisa Ataei: I discussed the Pendabot settings and configuration with, Kent a while back.

173
00:17:46.530 --> 00:18:03.030
Parisa Ataei: And the idea is that when it comes to… when it comes to updating packages because of a security risk, that's something that we should do immediately. Otherwise… and I don't know if other people in the team have felt the…

174
00:18:03.050 --> 00:18:07.779
Parisa Ataei: the pain that the Pentabot PRs are.

175
00:18:08.060 --> 00:18:15.770
Parisa Ataei: But to me, as long as things are working, updating them, upgrading packages every week, even,

176
00:18:16.260 --> 00:18:18.039
Parisa Ataei: That feels too much.

177
00:18:18.750 --> 00:18:34.869
Parisa Ataei: So, I wanted to get a sense of what the team thinks, and these are, Shielded doesn't have, really, a guideline on… on these configurations. I do not know what LFDT guidelines or requirements are.

178
00:18:35.180 --> 00:18:36.690
Parisa Ataei: I wonder if…

179
00:18:39.920 --> 00:18:42.960
Parisa Ataei: Kevin has an idea, if that'd look cool.

180
00:18:49.290 --> 00:18:52.270
Parisa Ataei: So, how do people feel about them?

181
00:19:01.050 --> 00:19:06.509
Kent Dybvig: Well, Priest, I think you know how I feel about them, but I will elaborate for anybody else who doesn't,

182
00:19:07.020 --> 00:19:11.320
Kent Dybvig: I like the Debian model, or the Red Hat model, where…

183
00:19:12.050 --> 00:19:20.860
Kent Dybvig: It's… you're always on a stable version that might actually be Way off the… the, cutting edge.

184
00:19:21.570 --> 00:19:25.090
Kent Dybvig: But that that stable version gets updated.

185
00:19:25.310 --> 00:19:35.279
Kent Dybvig: whenever there's a security problem or a bug that needs to be fixed, you just aren't getting new features. And then if for some individual package you need

186
00:19:35.880 --> 00:19:41.549
Kent Dybvig: The latest and greatest. Well, then that's… that's yours to install separately and maintain.

187
00:19:42.740 --> 00:19:46.950
Kent Dybvig: Unfortunately, I get the impression that's not the way this…

188
00:19:47.070 --> 00:19:49.980
Kent Dybvig: that… that the Rust world works.

189
00:19:50.240 --> 00:19:58.709
Kent Dybvig: And… I think it's unfortunate, because, but… and somebody, somebody in that world might… might…

